2.1.3.m:
that belongs to domestic animals;
in that he offers,
that possesses spiritual preeminence;
he accomplishes both.
Fallen away from this world,
not yet arrived at the world of the gods,
is the cooked oblation that is unanointed with clarified butter.
He illumines it;
he pours clarified butter over it;
thus he causes it to go to the gods.

He carries fire around it,
for the warding off of the Rakṣases.
Three times he carries fire around,
for the sacrifice is threefold,
and also for fitness for sacrifice.
If he were to remove it toward the east,
he would inflict the sacrificer with grief;
if toward the south,
it would belong to the realm of the Fathers;
if toward the west,

he would inflict the wife with grief.
He removes it toward the north;
this indeed is the peaceful direction of gods and men;
toward it he removes it for peace.
He makes a path,
for the continuity of the sacrifice.
He scorches it over the fire;
thereby he strews beneath it.
Four times he ladles it out;
four-footed are domestic animals;

one hands it to another,
such indeed is that;
he would be cut off from him.
In that he sets it over the Gārhapatya fire,
thereby he gratifies the Gārhapatya.
Agni was afraid:
'The oblations will pass beyond me.'
He saw this kindling stick;
he placed it upon [the fire];
thereupon indeed the oblations stayed in Agni.

2.2.11.m:
From the Ṣaḍḍhotṛ he fashioned the Saptahotṛ formula.
He employed it.
Upon its employment, he became a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer;
a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer was born to him.
Whoever should desire: 'May I be a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer;
may a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer be born to me,'
he should employ the Saptahotṛ formula.
He indeed becomes a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer;
a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer is born to him.
This domestic animal indeed stands firmly in five ways:

2.3.1.m:
Discussers of sacred lore say:
'What is the caturhotṛ-nature of the Caturhotṛ formulas?'
Because the hotṛ priests are fourfold in them,
therefore they are the Caturhotṛs;
therefore they are called Caturhotṛs.
That is the caturhotṛ-nature of the Caturhotṛ formulas.
Soma indeed is the Caturhotṛ;
Agni is the Pañcahotṛ;
Dhātṛ is the Ṣaḍḍhotṛ;
Indra is the Saptahotṛ;

2.3.2.m:
He exhausts his vital breaths.
If they should invite him back,
he should offer the Daśahotṛ formula in the Āgnīdhra fire itself
with ghee scooped four times,
sitting in the west facing east,
forwards and without dividing [the mantras];
he properly arranges the vital breaths for him.
One performing an expiation offers 'Vāc is the hotṛ' at the beginning of each season;
he properly arranges the seasons for him;
the seasons are properly arranged for him;

2.3.5.m:
he conquers all quarters.
Prajāpati is the hotṛ of the ten-hotṛ formula;
Soma is the hotṛ of the four-hotṛ formula;
Agni is the hotṛ of the five-hotṛ formula;
Dhātṛ is the hotṛ of the six-hotṛ formula;
Aryaman is the hotṛ of the seven-hotṛ formula.
These indeed are the hotṛs of the cāturhotṛ formulae.
He who knows them thus
eats food among all creatures;
he conquers all quarters.

2.3.7.m:
these of his self he rescues,
and goes to unison with Āditya.
Four times he scoops up;
twice he offers:
he obtains that rite of sacrifice which is the six-hotṛ,
the animal sacrifice;
the two breasts, the two testicles, the penis, and the downward breath—
these of his self he rescues,
and goes to unison with Āditya.
Four times he scoops up;
twice he offers;

2.4.1.m:
The conveyor of oblations, who overpowers malice, / the slayer of demons, victorious in battles, / luminous, shining, wise, / Agni, the maker of proper offerings [we invoke].
Accomplish that properly offered [rite], O Agni; / overcome all hostiles, O god; / shine out, showing us the broad path; / grant us luminous, unaging [life]!
To you, O Agni, the mortals bearing oblations / sing praise, to you the god;

From the ocean, from the far distance; / may the one blow skill to me, / may the other blow away whatever is ailment.
Whatever treasure of immortality / is placed there in your house, O wind, / therefrom give us to live; / therefrom grant us healing; / therefrom bring us greatness!
May the wind blow healing to us, / bringing welfare, bringing bliss to our heart;

2.4.6.m:
May the sons [of heaven] listen to our songs,
they who [are born] of truth;
may they be very gracious to us.
Today, divine Savitṛ,
send us good fortune with offspring;
drive away bad dreams.
Divine Savitṛ, drive away
all evils;
send us whatever is auspicious.
Bṛhaspati, pure, with songs of praise,

pay homage to at the sacrificial rites;
I celebrate his unyielding splendorous might.
Which the two gods of good skill, dakṣa's fathers, upheld
for asuric power, of eminent might.
He indeed dwells safely in his own home;
to him the sacrificial refreshment swells at all times;
to him the clans bow down of their own accord,
in whose kingdom the Brahmin walks in front.
With design, O Indra, with shaking,
with shaking, with buttock-shaking,

the desired, dear [portion] of the sacrifice.
"I give," says Agni;
"So be it," says Vāyu to that;
"Yes!" says the moon in truth;
the sun [says], "Indeed, truth, om!"
The waters brought that truth,
the dignity, the sacrificial gift of the sacrifice;
may this desire of mine be fulfilled.
For they found no other spy than him,
than Agni Vaiśvānara, as the forerunner;

the intermediate realm has spread wide;
the high heaven yields milk.
They are not lost, nor does the thief harm [them];
no hostile assailant assails them with distress;
and the master of cows with whom he worships the gods and gives [them],
with them he associates for a long time.
The dust-raising courser does not reach them;
they do not go toward slaughter;
along his broad-striding, fearless path,
the cows of that mortal sacrificer wander.

Night, approaching, has looked out
in many places with her eyes, the goddess;
she has assumed all glories.
Unto you, as [unto] cows, I have brought
a hymn of praise, O daughter of heaven;
Night, accept it like a victor.
The gods generated the divine Speech;
her the domestic animals of every form speak;
may she, the delightful cow Speech yielding to us refreshment and vigor,
well-praised, come unto us.

When Speech, speaking things incomprehensible,
the ruler of the gods, delightful, sat down,
she yielded as milk four invigorations, the waters;
whither indeed has her highest [form] gone?
The buffalo cow lowed, fashioning the floods,
becoming one-footed, two-footed, four-footed,
eight-footed, nine-footed,
thousand-syllabled in the highest firmament.
From her the oceans flow forth;
by that the four directions live;

from that flows the imperishable [syllable];
upon that the universe subsists.
Begetting the heroic Indras, Viśvakarman,
may he be with the Maruts, with a troop, with kin;
may the daughter-in-law, the co-wives, with mind
attend to the command, the speech of this father-in-law.
Indra, the hero, crossed the dust realms;
may this [man] be the father-in-law, the daughter-in-law, the co-wife;
may he conquer the enemies, exulting;
may he conquer prize in the winning of prize.
Agni, bearer of regal power, of unbent splendorous might,
possessed of thousands, may he blaze without neglect;
shining brightly, kindled, fierce,
he mounted the intermediate realm, he went to heaven.

2.4.7.m:
You are a bull shoot; you are drawn for the bull;
a bull is this fierce one, for the watcher of men;
heavenly, suitable for the rite, laid down, great indeed is the name
which is the hump of the bull.
May he become central, O Viṣṇu,
this one who is my bull;
then, like Indra to the gods,
may he proclaim to the people,
possessing a long lifespan, possessing lustre,
and the overlord of the clans.

He whose oblation this bull is,
led around for Indra,
shall conquer the approaching enemy,
and slays those who fight against him.
Truly he shall be the leader of men;
he shall be at the head of those who burst forth.
O Indra, stir up vigor in your body;
trample down all assailants;
crush down whatever distress is ours;
make wide space for us, O liberal giver.

Go forth, advance, bring forward, overpower;
do not turn away, make yourself heard among the people;
arise, praised, O bull, with your vigors;
O Indra, fight the enemies before us.
O Agni, conqueror, conquer you
the enemies with power, with splendorous might;
drive away the enemies, the foes.
This song of praise for you, O of mighty birth, I, an inspired poet,
have fashioned like a skilled craftsman a chariot, O wise one;
if indeed, O god Agni, you should welcome it,

2.5.2.m:
Rise up, you who possess prizes, who are within the waters;
enter this realm full of pleasant truth.
Rohita, who begot this universe,
may he establish us securely in realms.
Ascent after ascent Rohita mounted,
growth through offspring, the lap of births;
supported by them, he found the six wide regions;
foreseeing the path, you brought the realm here.
Rohita has brought the realm here;
he has dispersed the foes; may there be safety for us.

You, fierce Maruts, whose mother is Pṛśni,
crush the enemies together with Indra as your ally;
Rohita heard you, O heavenly ones,
thrice seven Maruts, rejoicing in sweetness.
Rohita begot heaven and earth;
in them Parameṣṭhin stretched out the warp;
in them Ajā Ekapād took his support;
he strengthened heaven and earth with power.
Rohita strengthened heaven and earth;
by him the sun is propped, by him the firmament.

He is the measurer of the space in the intermediate region;
through him the gods found the sun.
The flames shining upon you who are very gracious,
united as ladles, O Jātavedas,
sprinkle you, the prize-winner, with ghee;
you bring together nourishments, O Agni.
O Agni, be confident for great good fortune;
may your splendors be supreme;
make the household easy to guide;
trample upon the powers of those who act as enemies.

2.5.3.m:
the conqueror of enemies, observing all.
For intention, for desire, for fulfillment,
I place you in front, for immortality, for living.
For his intention, for assistance,
for the fulfillment of his desire,
they yoke the thoughts of Indra.
The divine Intention I place before the mind;
may the mother of the sacrifice be easy to invoke for me;
what I desire in my mind with longing,
may I find this residing in my heart.
